Consider the following program:

int main(void) {
    int a[5] = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10};
    int *p = a + 1;
    p += 2;
    printf("%d %d %d\n", *p, *(p - 1), p - a);
    (*p)++;
    printf("%d\n", a[3]);
    return 0;
}

Determine the exact output of the program. Explain how pointer arithmetic produces each printed value, and state what the expression p - a evaluates to and why.

The following function is intended to return the factorial of n but does not work correctly for all inputs:

int fact(int n) {
    int r;
    if (n = 0) return 1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        r = r * i;
    return r;
}

Identify every defect in this function, explain the consequence of each, and state which of them the compiler would warn about and which would only appear at run time.
